// The text of the Mānasollāsa's food chapter (Viṃśati 3, Adhyāya 13,
// "Annabhoga"), verses 1342–1600 — the complete section, ending at the
// colophon इत्यन्नभोगः before Adhyāya 14 on drinking water.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE TEXT. It is deliberately NOT generated from the recipe
// pages. Recipes carry their own shorter, cook-facing translations, and a
// digest of that kind is what used to live here — which is why whole verses
// had gone missing, most notoriously the nine-verse pig-cleaning procedure at
// 1427–1431 reduced to a single clause.
//
// The Sanskrit was collated character by character against the printed
// critical edition — Shrigondekar, ed., Mānasollāsa, Gaekwad's Oriental
// Series, Vol. II (Baroda, 1939), printed pages 115–136 — read from two
// independent scans:
//   https://www.ebharatisampat.in/pdfs/10403-Manasollasa.pdf
//   https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.208789
// Disputed glyphs were re-rendered at 400–1400 dpi; several corrections would
// have gone the wrong way at lower resolution. Every departure from the
// earlier working transcription is recorded verse by verse, with page
// citations, in research/manasollasa-collation-*.md.
//
// Conventions. Where the edition itself is corrupt, or the editor could not
// restore a reading, his sigla and parentheses are carried through rather than
// smoothed away, and the English says so in [square brackets]. Terms that are
// legible but unidentified stay transliterated and marked with *asterisks*.
// Nothing is emended by conjecture — several plausible emendations were tested
// against the page and lost.
//
// Refs are sense units, which frequently straddle verse boundaries, hence
// forms like "1385cd–1386ab". The source page links an entry to a recipe by
// overlapping verse range, so these still resolve.
export interface ManasollasaEntry {
  ref: string;
  latin: string;
  english: string;
}

