    english: "One should cook *śarkarā* mixed with water in a copper vessel. At the half-cooking one should throw in milk, or else *takra*, as one prefers; by this it releases its impurity, and that *śarkarā*, thus boiled, is the pure one. And one should strain it through a spotless cloth again and again. At the soft cooking it is fluid, a drink; at the middle, it resembles honey; at the hard, it is firm, a *bhakṣya*; and cooked beyond that, it becomes *śarkarā* [it grains back]. Out of *sitā* well perfected at the hard cooking, in a tight *sampuṭa*, the expert in *khaṇḍa*-cooking should make various forms. But into purified *sitā* he should mix an equal measure of milk, and boil that again up to the limit of the hard cooking. Taking it off the fire, and adding dry ginger, *tīkṣṇa*, cardamom, camphor, and saffron — having thrown these in, balls are to be made, and by name those are *varṣolakas*. [1412b reads `तक्रं`, buttermilk, which resolves the second clarifying agent: milk or buttermilk thrown into the half-cooked syrup carries the scum up. 1412d prints `सती`, \"genuine, pure\"; the following verses call the refined product `सिता`. 1414d reads `सिताया दृढसम्पुटे` — MS A has `पञ्चसम्पुटैः`; a *sampuṭa* is a closed two-part vessel, hence a mould, and this is a second product in the passage: hard-stage *sitā* moulded on its own, with no milk and no spice. 1416b reads `तावत्तत्क्वाथयेत्पुनः`, \"he should boil that again\". `खरपाकावधि`, \"up to the limit of the hard cooking\", is a stop instruction, and `साधिके शर्करा भवेत्` says what happens past it; the same terminal graining is stated of reduced milk at 1567.]",
