    english: "The flesh of the pig is unctuous; the flesh born of the *sāraṅga* [the spotted antelope] is fine. The meat of the *hariṇa* deer is wholesome; the flesh arising from the hare is dry. Sheep's flesh is invigorating and tasty; the flesh arising from the goat is light. Fish flesh is aphrodisiac; the flesh of birds is declared light. That which comes from the *ruru* and the *sambara* deer, if the animal is well-fed, is said to be tasty. Other meats are strengthening, and give pleasure to the tongue. [1417c: the edition prints `वराहपलळं`, with a doubled *la* (the second written ळ, as this volume regularly writes intervocalic *la*); normalized to ल it is `वराहपललं`, and the doubled syllable is what makes the pāda scan at eight. The sense is simply *varāha-palaṃ*, pig's flesh. Not emended. At 1418d the manuscripts read `क्रव्यमृ-` and the editor corrects to `क्रव्यम-`, giving `क्रव्यम् अजोद्भवम्`, \"the flesh arising from the goat\"; the corrected reading is printed here.]",
