    english: "That which lies alongside the backbone, both what is placed outside it and what is placed within; the dense lump of the haunch; that which arises at the root of the tail; that which arises in the region of the chest; the filling of the flank portion; that which lies in the two sides; the *vilepaka* at the joint of the belly; that which arises from the shoulder and the fore-quarter; the \"bud\" (*mukula*) that arises from the heart; the liver, likewise the kidney, the rectum-and-gut, and the entrails as well; the two eyes, the tongue and the two ears, *vṛdho*, the testicles and the *karṇaka*; and, closely attached to the animal's carcass, the caul-fat, the brain, and the marrow; the feet, the horns, the hooves, and the hide — this flesh is reckoned the finest. [At 1424c the manuscripts read `आस्यं`, \"mouth\", and the editor corrects to `अंस`, \"shoulder\"; the correction is printed here. At 1425d `वृधो` is obscure — MS A reads `बृधौ`, a dual, which would suit the paired organs on either side of it. At 1426ab the manuscripts read `वपामाष्ठिस्क-` and `मज्जं-`, with the editor's corrections `मास्तिष्क` [brain] and `माज`; the reading printed here follows his correction of the first and keeps `मज्जक` [marrow] for the second, which is what the metre and the sense require.]",
